      "commentary": "The answer to the opening question comes not as an object but as a relation, and the phrasing is one of the most quietly staggering in all of scripture: the ear of the ear, the mind of the mind, the eye of the eye. Whatever hears through your hearing, whatever knows through your mind — that is what you were asking about. It is not a further thing you could point to. It is the awareness that lends every faculty its power to function.\n\nHold the strangeness. Your ear does not hear; hearing happens in awareness, and the ear is only the instrument. A perfect ear on a corpse hears nothing. The mind does not, by itself, know; knowing is the light in which the mind's contents appear. So behind the eye there is a seeing that the eye serves; behind the mind, a knowing the mind serves. The Kena is pointing past every instrument to the one who is never an instrument — the witness that cannot itself be witnessed, because it is what does the witnessing.\n\nAnd then the turn: atimucya — releasing, letting go of \"all this,\" the whole apparatus of ear and eye and mind — the wise become immortal. Not by acquiring something new, but by loosening their grip on the instruments and recognizing themselves as the awareness that was operating them all along. Death, the verse implies, belongs to the instruments. What you actually are was never one of them."
