      "commentary": "Having named the awareness behind the senses, the Kena immediately admits the obvious problem: you cannot go there. The eye cannot travel to what enables seeing; the mind cannot reach what enables thought. Every instrument the text has just named is, by definition, disqualified from grasping its own source. Turn the eye inward as hard as you like — it will only ever find another image, never the seer.\n\nThen a line of rare and disarming honesty from a sacred teacher: na vidmaḥ na vijānīmaḥ — \"we do not know it, and we do not see how one could even teach it.\" The Upanishad confesses that its central subject cannot be handed over the way facts are handed over. This is not false modesty; it is precision about a genuine limit. Anything the mind can package and transmit is, for that very reason, an object — and the awareness in question is never an object. It is the light by which every object is known, and light cannot be lit.\n\nWhy build a whole text around what cannot be taught? Because there is a difference between transmitting information and pointing. The Kena cannot give you the Self. But it can keep turning your attention back from the seen toward the seer, again and again, until you notice what has been here the whole time. It cannot teach it. It can only, patiently, stop you from looking in the wrong direction."
