      "commentary": "Here is one of the most precise sentences ever written about the ultimate, and it works by closing both exits. It is other than the known — so it is not any fact, object, or experience you could add to your collection of things-you-know. But then, before you can retreat into comfortable mystery, the verse shuts that door too: and beyond the unknown. It is not merely an unknown lurking out there, waiting to be discovered like a distant planet. Neither category holds it.\n\nWhy does this matter so much? Because the mind only has two boxes: known and not-yet-known. Anything real, it assumes, must go in one or the other. The Kena's claim is that the awareness by which all knowing and not-knowing happen fits in neither, because it is the container, not the contents. You cannot know it as an object (it is the knower), and you cannot call it unknown (it is the most intimate, ever-present fact of your existence, nearer than any known thing). It is off the map — not because it is far away, but because it is the one drawing the map.\n\nAnd notice the closing humility, the same refrain the wisest verses keep returning to: iti śuśruma — thus we have heard, from those who saw. Even this razor-fine formulation is passed hand to hand, one careful mind to the next. The Kena states the most subtle point in its arsenal and immediately bows to its teachers. Confidence about the truth; humility about the source. That balance is itself part of the teaching."
