      "commentary": "Now the Kena delivers the same insight five times, once for each faculty, and the repetition is a meditation, not redundancy. Begin with speech. What cannot be spoken — yet is that by which all speaking happens? Every word you have ever uttered arose in an awareness that no word can capture. Language points at everything except the one pointing. So: know that as Brahman.\n\nBut it is the second line that gives this verse its edge, and it is startlingly bold for a religious text: na idam yad idam upāsate — \"not this, which people worship here.\" The Upanishad turns and gently dismantles the very religion surrounding it. Whatever image, deity, or concept people bow to as \"God\" — if it is a this, an object of worship you can hold before the mind — that is precisely not the ultimate. The moment the divine becomes a something you face and adore, it has become an object, and the real is never an object; it is the awareness in which even the worshipped image appears.\n\nThis is not a rejection of devotion. It is a warning against its subtlest failure: mistaking the finger for the moon, the symbol for the source, the concept of God for the living awareness that is closer than any concept. Five times the verse will name a faculty and say, in effect: the reality is not out there among the things you revere — it is the very seeing, speaking, hearing by which you revere anything at all. Turn around."
