      "commentary": "The second of the five, and the hardest to accept, because thought is where we most live. What the mind cannot think — but by which the mind is enabled to think at all. The mind, our most trusted tool, our very sense of being a competent self, is here quietly demoted: it is not the knower but a known, an instrument illumined by a deeper awareness it cannot itself grasp.\n\nTest it directly. Try to think the thinker. You will only ever produce another thought — an image of yourself thinking, a concept of the mind. The one to whom that thought appears has slipped away again, uncaught. The mind can think about anything in the universe except the awareness that is thinking, for the same reason a flashlight can illuminate the whole room but never its own bulb. Every attempt to mentally seize the Self generates one more object in consciousness, and consciousness itself stays serenely out of reach — not hidden, but too close, too much the seer to become the seen.\n\nThe practical fruit of this verse is a strange relief. So much suffering comes from believing the mind is the final court — that if the mind is anxious, I am in danger; if the mind is dark, I am lost. The Kena says: you are not the mind. You are the awareness in which the mind, with all its weather, appears and passes. The thoughts are known; you are the knowing. That single reorientation is the beginning of freedom from the tyranny of one's own head."
