      "commentary": "The third faculty: sight — and here the metaphor is at its most vivid. What the eye cannot see, but by which the eyes see. You have never once seen the seer. Look everywhere; you find only sights. The one looking is never among the objects looked at. And yet without it, no eye sees anything at all — the most perfect eye in darkness, or in death, sees nothing.\n\nThe eye is the classic image because its blind spot is literal. The eye cannot turn around and see itself seeing. It can see every other thing — galaxies, microbes, its own reflection in a mirror — but the reflection is still an object, an image, not the living act of seeing. The seer forever recedes behind the seen. This is not a flaw to be fixed; it is the structural signature of the Self. That which cannot appear in the field of experience, precisely because it is the one to whom the whole field appears — that, the Kena insists, is what you actually are.\n\nSo the instruction is not to see something new but to notice the seeing itself — the silent, ever-present awareness that has been looking out through your eyes since before you could name it, unchanged behind every changing scene. The world you see comes and goes. The seeing does not. Learn to rest your identity there, in the seer rather than the seen, and the whole restless drama of the visible loses its power to define you."
