      "name": "Tadvanam — the Thing Longed For",
      "devanagari": "तद्ध तद्वनं नाम तद्वनमित्युपासितव्यं स य एतदेवं वेदाभि\nहैनँ सर्वाणि भूतानि संवाञ्छन्ति ॥",
      "transliteration": "taddha tadvanaṁ nāma tadvanamityupāsitavyaṁ sa ya etadevaṁ vedābhi\nhainam̐ sarvāṇi bhūtāni saṁvāñchanti",
      "translation": "It is called Tadvanam — 'the thing longed for.' As Tadvanam should it be contemplated. Whoever knows it as such — toward that one, all beings yearn.",
      "words": [
        {
          "word": "tat ha tadvanam nāma",
          "meaning": "it is called Tadvanam — 'the longing for That' / 'the beloved of all'"
        },
        {
          "word": "tadvanam iti upāsitavyam",
          "meaning": "as Tadvanam it should be contemplated / adored"
        },
        {
          "word": "saḥ yaḥ etat evaṁ veda",
          "meaning": "whoever knows it thus"
        },
        {
          "word": "abhi enaṁ sarvāṇi bhūtāni saṁvāñchanti",
          "meaning": "toward that one, all beings yearn"
        }
      ],
      "commentary": "The Upanishad gives the ultimate a name to be held in the heart: Tadvanam — literally \"the longing for That,\" or \"that which is longed for.\" It is a name made of yearning. The reality behind all things is here identified as the secret object of every desire — the That toward which, knowingly or not, all longing is finally aimed. And it should be contemplated, adored, as exactly this: the beloved of all beings.\n\nThe insight folded into this name is profound and consoling. Every desire you have ever felt — for love, for beauty, for peace, for success, for rest — is, at its root, this one longing wearing a thousand masks. We chase the particular object, win it, and find the longing still unsatisfied, because no finite thing was ever what we were truly after. Beneath every craving is a homesickness for the source itself, the Tadvanam, the one thing whose attainment would end all seeking. To know this is to understand your own restless heart at last: it was always, in every wanting, wanting only That.\n\nAnd the promise: whoever knows the ultimate as Tadvanam — abhi enaṁ sarvāṇi bhūtāni saṁvāñchanti — toward that one, all beings yearn. The one who has recognized the beloved of all becomes, in some measure, transparent to it, and so all the yearning of the world flows naturally toward them, as rivers toward the sea. Not because they have made themselves desirable, but because they have become a clear window onto the one thing everything secretly desires. To rest in the source is to become a place where others feel, obscurely, the presence of what they too are seeking. The name to hold, then, is Tadvanam: the longed-for, at the end of every longing."
