      "commentary": "Near the end, a small dialogue that quietly names what the whole text has been. The student asks: teach me the Upanishad — the secret, the hidden connection, the innermost teaching. And the teacher answers: it has already been told you. brāhmīṁ vāva te upaniṣadam abrūma — we have told you the Upanishad of Brahman itself. There is no further, deeper secret being withheld. What you were straining toward has already been given.\n\nThe word upaniṣad itself means, roughly, \"the sitting-down-near\" — the intimate teaching received close to the teacher, and by extension the hidden equivalence at the heart of things: the secret that the awareness within you and the ground of all reality are one. The student, still hoping for some further esoteric revelation, is gently told that the secret has no hidden basement. The eye of the eye; the awareness behind the senses; the source the gods could not name; the lightning in the gap between thoughts; the Tadvanam longed for by all — that was the secret, and it has been spoken plainly.\n\nThere is a tender lesson in this exchange for every seeker who keeps waiting for the real teaching, the final key, the one more book or master that will finally deliver it. The teacher's answer is: it has been told. The truth is not being kept from you behind further veils; it is being overlooked in its very obviousness. The secret of the Upanishads is an open secret — the most intimate fact of your own being, hidden only by its nearness. What remains is not to learn more, but to recognize what has already, completely, been said. The last two verses name what such recognition rests on, and what it wins."
