    "34": {
      "id": 34,
      "ref": "4.8",
      "name": "Its Feet: Austerity, Restraint, Work",
      "devanagari": "तसै तपो दमः कर्मेति प्रतिष्ठा वेदाः सर्वाङ्गानि\nसत्यमायतनम् ॥",
      "transliteration": "tasai tapo damaḥ karmeti pratiṣṭhā vedāḥ sarvāṅgāni\nsatyamāyatanam",
      "translation": "Austerity, self-restraint, and work are its feet; the Vedas are all its limbs; truth is its dwelling place.",
      "words": [
        {
          "word": "tasyai tapaḥ damaḥ karma iti pratiṣṭhā",
          "meaning": "austerity, self-restraint, and action are its foundation / feet"
        },
        {
          "word": "vedāḥ sarva-aṅgāni",
          "meaning": "the Vedas are all its limbs"
        },
        {
          "word": "satyam āyatanam",
          "meaning": "truth is its dwelling place / abode"
        }
      ],
      "commentary": "The teaching has been given; now the Upanishad names the ground it must stand on. The secret knowledge is not a disembodied insight floating free of a life. It has feet — tapaḥ, damaḥ, karma: disciplined effort, self-restraint, and engaged action. It has limbs — the whole body of the Vedas, the tradition and its practices. And it has a home — satyam, truth, truthfulness, the living in accord with the real. Without these, the highest insight has nowhere to rest.\n\nThis verse is the Upanishad's safeguard against a familiar corruption: the person who collects lofty spiritual ideas while their actual life stays undisciplined, self-indulgent, and false. The Kena will not allow it. Realization, it insists, stands on feet — on the daily willingness to endure difficulty (tapas), to master one's impulses (dama), and to act rightly in the world (karma). It is clothed in the limbs of a real tradition and practice. And above all it dwells in truth: a person who lies, in speech or in life, has no house for this knowledge to live in, however much of it they can recite.\n\nThe balance here echoes the whole of the Upanishadic vision, and the Isha's insistence that world and spirit be held together. The most transcendent knowledge — the awareness beyond eye and mind, the source the gods could not name — must nonetheless be grounded in the most ordinary virtues: effort, restraint, honest action, plain truthfulness. Enlightenment does not float above the moral life; it stands on it, as a body stands on its feet. The loftiest recognition and the humblest discipline are not opposites. One is the head; the other is the ground it needs in order to stand at all."
    },
