      "commentary": "The Kena ends where the deepest teachings tend to end: not in ecstasy but in stability. Whoever knows it thus — having struck off all wrongdoing (apahatya pāpmānam) — stands firm, pratitiṣṭhati, in the boundless, glorious world. And the final word is repeated: pratitiṣṭhati, stands firm, stands firm — the doubling that traditionally marks a chapter's close, and here lands like feet settling onto solid ground.\n\nNotice what the fruit of all this subtle knowledge turns out to be. Not a fireworks display of visions, not powers, not escape — but firmness. To have recognized the awareness behind the senses, the source the gods could not name, is to have found at last something that cannot be shaken, and so to become unshakable oneself. The person who knows themselves as the changeless witness rather than the changing instruments has stopped standing on the shifting sand of circumstance and started standing on the one thing in existence that does not move. That is the whole promise: not that life stops changing, but that you stop being at its mercy.\n\nAnd the striking-off of wrongdoing comes first, as verse thirty-four insisted: the knowledge stands on truth and discipline, and in the light of the Self, what is false and harmful simply falls away — not through grim effort but because a mind established in its own depth no longer needs the small crookednesses that fear and grasping demand. So the shortest of the great inquiries closes on its quietest and most durable note. The eye of the eye has been found; the seeker has stopped charging at the truth and learned to rest in it; and the result is a person who stands firm, and stands firm, in the boundless — at home, at last, in what they always were."
